{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>ויעש.</b> The word וַיָסַר is strange grammatically, for in second-stem [hollow] verbs the first [root] letter is always with kamatz, like וַיָּשִׁב and וַיָּקָם. And if the last [root] letter were ח or ע or ר, which do not take a dagesh, the first letter would be with kamatz gadol, like וַיָּנַח בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי (below 20:11), וַיָּנַע לְבָבוֹ (Isaiah 7:2), וַיָּסַר אֵלֶיהָ (Judges 4 [—]) — and these are from the light binyan [pa'al], for in the heavy binyan [pi'el] the first letter would be with kamatz qatan if the last letter were not one of the mentioned letters, like וַיָּשֶׁב אֶת כָּל־הָרְכֻשׁ (Genesis 14:16). Now, וַיָּסַר comes in place of וַיָּסֶר, like וַיָּסַר אֶת אוֹפַן מַרְכְּבֹתָיו (below 14:25). And it is correct that וְסַר הֶעָרֹב is from the light binyan, like וְסַר הֶעָרֹב (Exodus [—])."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra cites Judges 4 without specifying the verse; the phrase וַיָּסַר אֵלֶיהָ does not appear in Judges 4 in any standard text, so the reference may be to a different passage or a textual variant. The closing parenthetical citation 'פ׳ כה' is ambiguous but likely refers to the a-mnemonic sign for וְסַר הֶעָרֹב, i.e. Exodus 8:27 (פרשת וארא), the verse under comment."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:32:55.745848299Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}